sections.s2_technical:
The first contributor is the thread stack. In the default thread-per-request model (Tomcat), each worker thread has a stack whose reserved size is controlled by `-Xss`, commonly defaulting near one megabyte. This memory is off-heap (native) and is reserved per thread, not per request, but since a request occupies one thread for its duration, it is fair to attribute a thread's stack to the in-flight request. A pool of 200 Tomcat threads therefore reserves roughly 200 megabytes of stack regardless of heap.

The second contributor is the request object graph. When a JSON body arrives, Jackson deserializes it into your DTO. For a transfer request that is small, but for a batch endpoint that accepts a list of 10,000 trades, the deserialized `List<TradeRequest>` can be many megabytes on the heap. These objects live until the request completes and then become garbage.

The third contributor is everything the business logic allocates: entities loaded from the database, intermediate collections, streams, string concatenations, and mapping between entities and DTOs. A report endpoint that loads 50,000 transactions for Priya's CUR3001 to compute a statement can allocate a large transient object graph that dwarfs the request and response.

The fourth contributor is JSON serialization. When Spring writes the response, Jackson walks the object graph and produces bytes. During that process both the live object graph and the growing byte buffer exist at once, so the transient peak is roughly the object graph plus the serialized bytes, which is why people say "about twice the payload." Streaming the response (for example with a `StreamingResponseBody` or Jackson's streaming API) avoids holding the whole byte array in memory.

Now measurement. The heap-diff method is the most direct. You force a garbage collection, read used heap, run a known number N of identical requests, force GC again, read used heap, and divide the difference by N for a rough per-request retained estimate. In code you use `ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean().getHeapMemoryUsage().getUsed()` around the loop.

```java
System.gc();
long before = ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean().getHeapMemoryUsage().getUsed();
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) service.generateStatement("CUR3001");
System.gc();
long after = ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean().getHeapMemoryUsage().getUsed();
long perRequest = (after - before) / 1000;   // rough retained bytes per request
```

The Actuator method uses production endpoints. With `spring-boot-starter-actuator` and Micrometer you expose `/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used`, `jvm.gc.pause`, and `jvm.gc.memory.allocated`. The last one, allocation rate, divided by request throughput gives bytes allocated per request including garbage, which is often more useful than retained memory because high allocation drives GC pressure.

```java
@Bean
MeterRegistryCustomizer<MeterRegistry> tags() {
    return reg -> reg.config().commonTags("service", "statement-api");
}
// GET /actuator/metrics/jvm.gc.memory.allocated → total allocated; divide by request count
```

The AOP method instruments request boundaries. You write an aspect around controller methods that samples used heap before and after each call and records the delta to a timer or distribution summary. This gives per-endpoint attribution so you can see that the statement endpoint allocates far more than the balance endpoint.

```java
@Aspect @Component
class MemoryProfilingAspect {
    private final MemoryMXBean mem = ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean();
    @Around("execution(* com.bank.*.controller.*.*(..))")
    public Object profile(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        long before = mem.getHeapMemoryUsage().getUsed();
        try { return pjp.proceed(); }
        finally {
            long delta = mem.getHeapMemoryUsage().getUsed() - before;
            // record delta against pjp.getSignature() to Micrometer
        }
    }
}
```

A senior nuance: per-request measurement is noisy because many requests run concurrently and GC runs asynchronously, so AOP deltas are samples, not exact accounting. The reliable signals are allocation rate and retained heap under steady load. Also prefer streaming large responses, avoid loading entire result sets, and use pagination so no single request holds a huge object graph.

